    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Private Boat Trip or Yacht Charter in Madeira: What Is the Difference?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>26 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">A private boat trip is a few hours on a small boat with a skipper, only your group on board, along a set stretch of coast. A yacht charter usually means a larger boat with cabins and a crew, hired for a whole day or longer, and for most visitors to Madeira it is more boat than the day needs.</p>

      <h2>Is a yacht just a bigger boat?</h2><p>Mostly, yes. In booking listings there is no fixed length at which a boat becomes a yacht, so the useful difference is what the boat lets you do: a few hours along the cliffs, or a long day with cabins, a crew and room to spread out.</p><p>The word is stretched both ways in Madeira. A small open motor boat may be sold as a "yacht tour", and a large catamaran may sell single seats to strangers. So read past the headline. Three details tell you what you are really booking: how many guests the boat takes, what is below deck, and how long the hire lasts.</p><h2>How do the two compare, line by line?</h2><p>Here is a plain side by side, with our two trips in one column and a typical crewed yacht charter in the other.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Private boat trip (Chifbay)</th><th>Crewed yacht charter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who is on board</td><td>Your group only, up to 5 guests</td><td>Your group, often a larger one</td></tr><tr><td>Below deck</td><td>A bathroom</td><td>Usually a saloon, cabins and one or more bathrooms</td></tr><tr><td>Crew</td><td>A local professional skipper</td><td>A skipper, often with a deckhand, host or cook</td></tr><tr><td>Length of hire</td><td>2 h, 2 h 30 or 3 h</td><td>Usually a whole day, several days or a week</td></tr><tr><td>How far</td><td>Funchal west to Ribeira Brava or Ponta do Sol</td><td>Further, such as the Desertas or Porto Santo, when the sea allows</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>400 to 600 EUR for the whole boat</td><td>Priced by the day or the week, in a much higher bracket</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The middle column is all that we sell. The right hand column varies a great deal from boat to boat, so treat it as a sketch, not a quote.</p><h2>What does the extra size buy you?</h2><p>Space, steadiness and range. A longer, heavier hull moves less in a swell, has room to walk about and somewhere to lie down out of the sun, and can cross open water that a small boat should leave alone.</p><p>Those are real gains, but they matter most on long days and long distances. West of Funchal the coast is short and packed. Measured along the water from the marina mouth, Câmara de Lobos is about 6.9 km away, the sea under Cabo Girão about 9.7 km, and Fajã dos Padres about 11.7 km. The island's high ground also shelters this side from the usual north east wind, which is why <a href="/posts/why-is-madeira-south-coast-calmer-for-boats">the south coast is the calmer side for small boats</a>.</p><p>A small boat has strengths of its own. It sits low to the water, which suits swim stops. It can idle slowly at the foot of a cliff and then move on briskly when the stop is done. And with five guests at most, everyone is within talking distance of everyone else, the skipper included.</p><h2>Do you need a cabin for a few hours at sea?</h2><p>Usually not. A cabin earns its place when you sleep on board, change in private on a long day or shelter from weather on a passage. On a morning that starts at 10:00 and ends at 13:00, people rarely miss a cabin. They miss a toilet and some shade.</p><p>Both are on our boat: there is a bathroom on board, and a canopy over the seats. Food is served on board, and so are poncha, beer, soft drinks and a bottle of local wine, so there is no need for a galley either. What you take home is covered as well: an Insta360 360° video of the trip, photos from a real camera and, when the weather allows, drone footage from above.</p><h2>How far can each one go?</h2><p>Distance is where a charter earns its price. Our <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip along the south coast</a> runs out past the harbour of Câmara de Lobos and the face of Cabo Girão, with a swim below the Fajã dos Padres cliffs and another one off Ribeira Brava. The 3 hour version goes on to Ponta do Sol, a little under 19 km from the marina in a straight line. That is the classic south coast run, start to finish.</p><p>Past it, the open Atlantic begins. The Desertas Islands lie about 22 nautical miles, roughly 41 km, south east of Funchal, and Porto Santo about 71 km to the north east. Both crossings mean hours of passage, more exposure to swell and a close watch on the forecast. The Desertas are also a nature reserve: any visit needs prior authorisation from IFCN, the regional nature institute, and the bay at Doca is the only authorised anchorage. If one of those islands is the point of your trip, a larger boat on a longer hire is the right tool. Our guide to <a href="/posts/can-you-go-to-porto-santo-by-boat-from-madeira">reaching Porto Santo by boat</a> explains why most visitors take the ferry.</p><h2>Who should book a yacht charter instead?</h2><p>Some groups truly need more boat, and it is better to say so plainly.</p><ul><li><strong>Six or more people.</strong> Chifbay takes 5 guests at most, so a larger party needs a larger boat.</li><li><strong>A night on board,</strong> or a whole day or several days at sea.</li><li><strong>The Desertas or Porto Santo</strong> as the goal of the day.</li><li><strong>An event with a crowd,</strong> where people need room to stand, mingle and sit down at a table.</li><li><strong>Sailing for its own sake.</strong> If the engine off and the boat heeling is what you are after, read <a href="/posts/can-you-go-sailing-in-madeira">whether a sailboat suits this coast</a>.</li></ul><h2>What do most visitors actually need?</h2><p>For a couple, a family or a few friends with a free morning or evening, a few hours on a small private boat fits Madeira well. Five questions settle it quickly.</p><ol><li><strong>How many of you are there?</strong> Five or fewer, a small private boat works. More than five, look at larger boats.</li><li><strong>How long do you want to be out?</strong> Two to three hours covers the cliffs, a swim or the sunset. A day or more points to a charter.</li><li><strong>Do you want to swim?</strong> The Day Trip has two swim stops and paddle boards. The <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> has no swimming, and leaves 1 h 15 before that day's sunset.</li><li><strong>Does anyone get seasick?</strong> A short run on the sheltered south coast is a gentler test than a long crossing. Our <a href="/posts/seasickness-boat-trip-madeira-guide">seasickness guide</a> has the details.</li><li><strong>Is the operator licensed?</strong> Companies selling trips at sea in Portugal must be listed in the RNAAT, the national register run by Turismo de Portugal. Chifbay is registered as a maritime tourism operator under RNAAT n.º 305/2026, and our guide to <a href="/posts/how-to-check-a-boat-tour-operator-in-madeira-is-licensed">checking that an operator is licensed</a> shows how to look any company up.</li></ol><p>If your answers point to the small boat, you can compare both options on our page of <a href="/experiences">private trips from Funchal</a>. If they point to a bigger one, that is a fair outcome too. A good day at sea starts with the right size of boat.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is a yacht more comfortable than a small boat in Madeira?</h3><p>On a long day or in a swell, a larger yacht usually moves less and has more room. For two or three hours on the sheltered south coast, the difference matters far less, and a small boat makes swim stops simpler.</p><h3>Can a private boat in Madeira take more than five people?</h3><p>Some boats can, but Chifbay's trips take 5 guests at most. A group of six or more should look at a larger boat.</p><h3>Are food and drinks included on a private boat trip?</h3><p>On Chifbay trips they are: food, plus a choice of poncha, beer, soft drinks or local wine. On a yacht charter, catering is often agreed separately, so ask what the price covers.</p><h3>Can a private boat trip reach the Desertas Islands?</h3><p>Chifbay's trips stay on the south coast between Funchal and Ponta do Sol. The Desertas are an open sea crossing of about 41 km, with a visit that needs IFCN authorisation, so they are a job for a longer hire on a larger boat.</p>

      <blockquote>Choose the boat for the day you want, not for the word on the listing.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

